# minecraft fix log

## epoch 0 (2026-11-20): created from a reference reel
Made by Claude from references/minecraft.mp4: a 4.0 s, 1080x1920, 30 fps, silent, single-shot clip rendered from the /minecraft skill of github.com/artemnovitckii/claude-animation-skills (MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2026 Artem Novitckii), and from that skill's source (skills/minecraft/SKILL.md and template.html, three.js). Analysis: reference-lab/runs/minecraft/analysis-claude (survey and review answered by Claude), full-resolution crops in reference-lab/runs/minecraft/crops and /detail, committed copy in reference-lab/refs/minecraft. The template is named after the creator's skill (user's choice), not after the kind.

What the video gave:
- One continuous shot, no cuts (rea-video: 1 shot, change curve 00000100, no audio track). A small building assembles itself on a terraced grassy hill: stone foundation ring, plank floor, three courses of plank walls with log corners and glass panes, a stepped dark-plank roof row by row, then the last block slams in at 2.7 s with a dust puff and a camera shake, the panes light up warm at 2.8 s, smoke cubes rise; the camera orbits about 57 degrees, cranes down from about 29 to 11 degrees and pushes in, ending on a front three-quarter hero frame.
- Look: rea-video found no pixel grid (smooth render, nearest-filtered 16 x 16 textures, texels about 4 to 5 px), flat fills 34%, gentle gradients 26%, 82 colours for 95% of the frame, background detail 7.16. Measured colours (tools/oklch_colours.py): grass L 0.52 to 0.67, C 0.12 to 0.15, H 132 to 137; sky L 0.78, C 0.10, H 264 at mid height; dirt L 0.49, C 0.06, H 65; roof dark brown L 0.23 to 0.31, H 45 to 66; title white L 0.98 with a near-black warm shadow L 0.27 to 0.29.
- Type: a lowercase white pixel-font line at the top (92 px, y about 345) whose letters drop in one by one; at the slam a one-word line at 150 px slams in with a scale overshoot; hard dark drop shadows, no panels.

What the source code gave (exact, converted to OKLCH relations by role; the hex values stay in the source):
- The 16 x 16 procedural textures: grass top (6 greens, weighted), grass side (dirt with a green drip 3 to 5 texels deep), dirt (6 browns plus 4% grey pebbles), cobblestone (10 jittered Voronoi points, mortar where the second-nearest minus nearest distance is under 1.15 texels), planks and dark planks (4 boards, a seam row, one butt joint per board), log (bark stripes per column, ring end grain), leaves (13% holes), glass (pale frame, diagonal glints, 18% fill), lantern light (warm ramp).
- The block motion: fall 0.2 s from 2.4 blocks up, scale 0.5 to 1, small spin; landing hop of 0.2 block over 0.16 s; squash 0.22 * e^(-15 v) * cos(36 v), x widening 0.45 of it; the last block falls 0.23 s from 5.5 blocks with squash 0.4. The build order: a ring sorted by angle, the floor from its centre outward, each wall course by angle, roof rows snaking left and right, layers overlapping by a few hundredths of a second. Dust puff (36 cubes, outward with drag, gravity, shrinking over 0.5 to 0.85 s), smoke (12 cubes, 1.25 s life, rising and drifting, grey to white), lights switched on in 3 steps (0.35, 0.7, 1, 0.04 s apart) with a flicker, camera shake 0.2 * e^(-13 t).
- The title: 0.03 s letter stagger, each letter falls 70 px in 0.12 s then hops 14 px, positions snapped to 6 px steps; the second word scales 1.6, 0.86, 1.07, 1 within 0.16 s; shadow offset 7 px at 92 px (10 px at 150).
- Sky gradient (top L 0.67, C 0.15, H 260 to horizon L 0.86, C 0.06, H 257), flat slab clouds on a coarse grid, oak trees (log column, 5 x 5 leaf layers, a 3 x 3 layer, a plus on top).

Not taken: the topic and its objects (a cottage with a chimney), its words ("block by block", "home."), and any Mojang texture, logo or character (the skill draws its own textures in code; so does the kit).

Translation decisions (the engine is a 2D canvas: M3 writes a kit, 2 or 3 assets and one draw() per shot):
- No 3D: a fixed oblique three-quarter FRONT view instead of an orbiting perspective camera. Front face a 64 px square, one step of depth = 20 px right and 24 px up; top and right faces are parallelograms; 4 px texels mapped with ctx.transform; painter's order z descending, y ascending, x ascending; hidden-face culling. Prototyped in the engine's own stage with the CPU canvas the validator uses (scratchpad mc_proto): about 140 textured blocks plus the hill background and titles drew in 35 to 77 ms per frame (the validator's limit is 180 ms), and the frames read as the reference's world. Why not isometric: a 2:1 isometric ground covering 1080 x 1920 needs about 1,100 textured cells and has no horizon; the front view keeps the reference's sky, horizon fog and horizontal grass terraces (cheap speckled bands with dirt-face edges), and its front faces are plain squares.
- Camera: no orbit; every build shot pushes in 6% with a 20 to 30 px drift, the hook pulls back, the slam shakes.
- Pixel grid: not used. The reference is a smooth render (no frame-level grid measured), and its texels sit at the art-pixel scale, so with the engine's slow per-shot zoom every texel would swim against a 6 px grid. Texels are exact 4 px rectangles instead; text stays crisp anyway.
- Font: the engine has no pixel font (the source uses Pixelify Sans). kit.title uses bold Consolas, lowercase, a hard drop shadow (ctx.shadow, blur 0) and the source's per-letter drop; a hand-typed bitmap font would risk garbled letters and bypass the engine's text checks.
- Colour: oklch() strings work in the stage canvas (tested: oklch(0.63 0.145 136) paints rgb 94, 156, 60), so materials are given as OKLCH ramps and faces are shaded by lightness offsets (top +0.05, right -0.12); h.mix only reads hex, which the look section states.
- Length: one 4 s build becomes a 30 to 50 s narrated explainer with one named part per shot: hook (the finished build, alive), unit (the thing it is made of), base, 2 to 4 parts, optional inside cutaway, finish (the finishing block slams in, lamps light, payoff word), optional stats. Every part shot carries one real number with its unit, the same kind of quantity for every part.
- Sound: the reference is silent; narrator will (friendly young American man), music Bright Bouncy Uplifting 100 to 120 bpm peaking at the finish slam; effects: a knock per part, a boom on the slam, pops on stat cards.

Engine limits met (not changed):
- The engine always narrates and draws its own caption box at y about 1520 to 1680 (dark box, accent bar = palette.accent); a template cannot change its look, so the reference's caption-free top-title frame gains a caption over the bottom terraces. palette.accent is set to the lamp gold.
- The engine adds a cut smear at every shot start; the reference is one continuous build. Each shot opens with the earlier parts already standing, so the build reads as continuous.
- The kit helpers beyond the contract (block, voxels, fall, cube, MAT) are not tested by the kit validator except through kit.bg, which the template tells to draw its terraces and trees with them; other bugs in them surface only in asset and shot validation.
- The plan prompt asks for 40 to 90 word asset descriptions; a block model needs more, so the template asks for a compact part list.
- The asset validator rejects an asset whose first preview state draws nothing, so the build's first state is the finished build.

First brief: not run yet (the main session runs the reels).

epoch 1: pyramid-mc-e1 ("how the Great Pyramid was built") FAILED before the voice (ElevenLabs quota exhausted: 0 of 10000 credits left), so no video; shots were made and validated first.
- Flaw: shots 1 and 4 to 7 shipped failing "too slow" (239 to 424 ms a frame) and "not deterministic". Measured in the stage on the reel's own kit: kit.bg "hill" alone takes 177 ms a frame (dense speckles, textured terrace blocks and two voxel trees drawn texel by texel every frame); shot 4 on top of it 199 ms; both deterministic when rendered alone (the "not deterministic" rejections came with the slow frames). -> fix: kit.bg's static layers are drawn once inside makeKit into an OffscreenCanvas per background ("hill", "close"); each frame draws that image plus the moving clouds only.
- Flaw: shot 1's empty panel at y 410 to 506 (a panel shown before its number) -> left to the existing panel rule for now; check on e2.

epoch 2: pyramid-mc-e2 (same brief, Kokoro voice am_liam: ElevenLabs out of credits; 39.8 s, 8 shots, MiniMax $0.223, reel total $0.224)
- The cached background fixed the speed (no "too slow" rejections), but this run's kit drew every block as one flat orange face: its matColor returned the same colour for every texel run, so trees, terraces and the build lost their textures (measured: background detail 3.3 against 7.5 for e1's textured kit). The kit validator passed it because nothing checked texture -> `background detail: 5` added to ## checks.
- The build barely showed (the hero "build" at 2 to 8% of the frame in every shot, 20+ rejections): with flat untextured blocks the pyramid read as a tiny orange square; re-check on e3 now that textures are enforced.
- Engine: new h.cache(key, w, hgt, draw) (the hill is now cached through it): h.rr / h.poly draw on the offscreen canvas while it runs (a raw OffscreenCanvas would have sent their paths to the frame instead). The SPEED rule now uses it.
- Lines: decent order (foundation, core, chambers, upper core, casing, capstone) with real numbers (146 m, 2.3 million, 230 m, 51 degrees 50 minutes); "twenty-five-sixty BC" reads badly -> left to the planner's number-to-words rule.
- Voice: Kokoro am_liam pitch spread 3.91 st (LOW, gate 4), as expected for Kokoro.
